# 11. The Recursion Pattern

Remember this:

```text
merge_sort()
      ↓
   Split
   /   \
Left   Right
 ↓       ↓
Sort    Sort
 \       /
   Merge
```

This is the main idea behind Merge Sort.

---

# 12. Base Case

This line is extremely important:

```python
if head is None or head.next is None:
    return head
```

Why?

A list containing:

```text
one node
```

is already sorted.

Example:

```text
5
```

No further splitting is needed.

---

# 13. Complexity

For `N` nodes:

| Complexity  | Value                     |
| ----------- | ------------------------- |
| Time        | O(N log N)                |
| Extra Space | O(log N) due to recursion |

The merging itself uses **O(1) extra node storage** because we reuse existing nodes.

---

# 14. Merge Sort vs Array Sorting

For linked lists, Merge Sort is especially useful because:

* We don't need random access.
* Finding the middle can be done with slow/fast pointers.
* Nodes can be rearranged by changing links.
* The algorithm has predictable `O(N log N)` time.

